schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Internal capacity"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A community's ability to do things independently, without relying on
    external experts, consultants, or hired help — the skills, knowledge,
    and systems that live within the members themselves, spanning technical,
    governance, communication, and financial domains.
  source_traditions:
    - community organizing / capacity-building practice (general)
    - web3-specific operational skills (wallet, smart contract, treasury, security)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Framed in the article as the alternative to permanent dependence on
    external experts — the five-step build process (identify gaps, map
    existing skills, create learning pathways, practice with low stakes,
    document everything) is presented as how a node closes the gap over
    time.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "'capacity building' in international-development discourse (a broader, often institution-to-institution framing)"
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      the article separates "what can you do now" from "what can't you do"
      as the diagnostic starting point, rather than assuming capacity gaps
      are obvious.
  tensions: []
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating capacity-building as a one-time training event rather than an
    ongoing five-step cycle (gap → skill map → pathway → low-stakes practice
    → documentation) loses the article's framing of it as continuous
    infrastructure work, not a single workshop.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-internal-capacity.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-b3a05e9b7743
  source_lineage: building-internal-capacity.md
